
# Set up
# Level 1 credits is the first little endian 32 bit value.  0x00286bee is 4000000000 in little-endian.
# This is deliberately huge.  Credits per level credit window can never exceed 6*L/N tenths, where L is the lifetime
# level requirement (about 49.26 * 10 * level_1_credits) and N is the number of windows per year.  The test program's
# short window (LEVEL_CREDIT_WINDOW_SECONDS in test/program/common) makes N very large (525600 for 60 second windows),
# so with a small level_1_credits every contribution earns 0 credits no matter how large it is, and the credit checks
# would compare 0 with 0.  With this value, even a 1 Ki contribution earns many tenths of a credit.  As a consequence
# the entry never actually reaches level 1 in these tests.
# The ki_factor for levels appears as an unsigned little-endian 32 bit value at offset 76 + (level * 308).
# 0xf4240000 is 9460 in little-endian.
# Also set the names to the level.  This is used to ensure that metaplex metadata is being updated.  The first
# character of the name is at 80 + (level * 308).
# The last 4 bytes, at offset 2840, are ki_harvest_reduction_per_day, which is 0 (Ki harvests are never reduced).
# The expected annual reward rate, which calibrates the level credit curve, is the block's: 490 basis points (4.90%
# per year), given when block 16 0 is created below.

METADATA=`(echo 0x00286bee | xxd -r | tr -d '\n';                                                                    \
           dd if=/dev/zero bs=1 count=64 status=none;                                                                \
           for i in \`seq 0 8\`; do                                                                                  \
               dd if=/dev/zero bs=1 count=8 status=none;                                                             \
               echo 0xf4240000 | xxd -r | tr -d '\n';                                                                \
               printf "%x" $i;                                                                                       \
               dd if=/dev/zero bs=1 count=295 status=none;                                                           \
           done;                                                                                                     \
           dd if=/dev/zero bs=1 count=4 status=none) | base64 | tr -d '\n'`
METADATA_PIECE1=`echo "$METADATA" | base64 -d | dd bs=1 count=900 status=none           | base64 | tr -d '\n'`
METADATA_PIECE2=`echo "$METADATA" | base64 -d | dd bs=1 count=900 status=none skip=900  | base64 | tr -d '\n'`
METADATA_PIECE3=`echo "$METADATA" | base64 -d | dd bs=1 count=900 status=none skip=1800 | base64 | tr -d '\n'`
METADATA_PIECE4=`echo "$METADATA" | base64 -d | dd bs=1           status=none skip=2700 | base64 | tr -d '\n'`
# Entry 2 gets the same metadata except that its level 1 credits is 1 (0x01000000 in little-endian).  As explained
# above, with the test program's short window no contribution to such an entry can earn even a tenth of a credit,
# which is what the user_level_up_no_credits test needs.  Only the first four bytes differ, so only the first piece
# differs.
METADATA_NO_CREDITS=`(echo 0x01000000 | xxd -r | tr -d '\n';                                                         \
                      echo "$METADATA" | base64 -d | dd bs=1 status=none skip=4) | base64 | tr -d '\n'`
METADATA_NO_CREDITS_PIECE1=`echo "$METADATA_NO_CREDITS" | base64 -d | dd bs=1 count=900 status=none                 \
                            | base64 | tr -d '\n'`
# Entry 3 gets metadata whose level credit curve is flat from the very first Ki, so that applying more than 1 Ki earns
# no more credits than applying 1 Ki does; this is what the user_level_up_excess_not_burned test needs.  The curve is
# P(k) = 240*L*k / (factor*bps + 40*N*k) tenths of a credit (see level_credit_reference below).  With ki_factor 1 and
# the block's expected_annual_reward_bps of 490, factor*bps is 490, which is negligible next to 40*N*k (more than 21
# million for every k >= 1, with the test program's short window), so P(k) is 6*L/N rounded down for every k >= 1.
# level_1_credits 10000 (0x10270000 in little-endian) makes that 56 tenths of a credit, far below the 100000 tenths
# that level 1 requires.
METADATA_FLAT=`(echo 0x10270000 | xxd -r | tr -d '\n';                                                               \
                dd if=/dev/zero bs=1 count=64 status=none;                                                           \
                for i in \`seq 0 8\`; do                                                                             \
                    dd if=/dev/zero bs=1 count=8 status=none;                                                        \
                    echo 0x01000000 | xxd -r | tr -d '\n';                                                           \
                    printf "%x" $i;                                                                                  \
                    dd if=/dev/zero bs=1 count=295 status=none;                                                      \
                done;                                                                                                \
                dd if=/dev/zero bs=1 count=4 status=none) | base64 | tr -d '\n'`
METADATA_FLAT_PIECE1=`echo "$METADATA_FLAT" | base64 -d | dd bs=1 count=900 status=none           | base64 | tr -d '\n'`
METADATA_FLAT_PIECE2=`echo "$METADATA_FLAT" | base64 -d | dd bs=1 count=900 status=none skip=900  | base64 | tr -d '\n'`
METADATA_FLAT_PIECE3=`echo "$METADATA_FLAT" | base64 -d | dd bs=1 count=900 status=none skip=1800 | base64 | tr -d '\n'`
METADATA_FLAT_PIECE4=`echo "$METADATA_FLAT" | base64 -d | dd bs=1           status=none skip=2700 | base64 | tr -d '\n'`
SALT0=0
SALT1=1
SALT2=2
SALT3=3
SHA2560=`compute_metadata_sha256 $METADATA $SALT0`
SHA2561=`compute_metadata_sha256 $METADATA $SALT1`
SHA2562=`compute_metadata_sha256 $METADATA_NO_CREDITS $SALT2`
SHA2563=`compute_metadata_sha256 $METADATA_FLAT $SALT3`

